You can create a custom exception class and register an exception handler for it using the `@app.exception_handler()` decorator. For example, if you have a custom exception `UnicornException`, you can define it and register a handler as follows:

```python
class UnicornException(Exception):
    pass

@app.exception_handler(UnicornException)
async def unicorn_exception_handler(request: Request, exc: UnicornException):
    return JSONResponse(
        status_code=418,
        content={"message": "Oops! {name} did something. There goes a rainbow!".format(name=request.path_params["name"])},
    )
```

This handler will be called whenever a `UnicornException` is raised anywhere in your application, and it will return a response with a status code of 418 and a custom message.
